
			    PGP VERIFICATION OF CONTROL MESSAGES

    Newgroup, rmgroup and checkgroups control messages for the big
    hierarchies are signed by their maintainers.  The signature travels in
    the X-PGP-Sig header, created by "signcontrol".  dreaderd only acts on
    such a message when dcontrol.ctl says "verify-<signer>" and the
    signature is good and was made by that signer's key.

    The check is done by dbin/pgpverify with GnuPG's gpgv, the
    verify-only part of GnuPG.  gpgv 1.x (GnuPG 1.4) and 2.x both work.
    PGP 2.x can still be used, see "PGP 2.x" below.

				UPGRADING

    Before this version, pgpverify ran PGP 2.x ("pgp") unless
    pgpverifyargs in diablo.config named another program.  Now gpgv is the
    default.  A site that verified control messages with PGP 2.x and has no
    pgpverifyargs line must create a gpgv keyring (see "THE KEYRING" below) before
    upgrading; until it exists every verify-<signer> control message is
    refused (fail closed).  To keep PGP 2.x, set pgpverifyargs as shown in
    "PGP 2.x" below.

    pgpverify also refuses, as unverifiable, an article in which a signed
    header (or X-PGP-Sig) occurs more than once in any letter case, or that
    contains a NUL byte or a bare CR, and dreaderd never executes a control
    message with Control:, From:, Newsgroups: or X-PGP-Sig: twice.  Such
    messages are not produced by signcontrol.

				REQUIREMENTS

    Install the package that provides gpgv.  pgpverify looks for gpgv2,
    gpgv and gpgv1 in /usr/bin, /usr/local/bin, /opt/local/bin,
    /usr/pkg/bin and /opt/ooce/bin, in that order.  gpgv2 comes first
    because where both are installed (pkgsrc's gnupg and gnupg2), gpgv
    is 1.4, which can't check newer key types such as Ed25519.
    Examples:

	FreeBSD		pkg install gnupg	(gpgv 2.x)
			pkg install gnupg1	(gpgv 1.4; conflicts with gnupg)
	SmartOS/pkgsrc	pkgin install gnupg2	(gpgv2, 2.x; recommended)
			pkgin install gnupg	(gpgv 1.4)
	Debian/Ubuntu	apt install gpgv	(gpgv 2.x, usually present)
			apt install gpgv1	(gpgv1, 1.4)
	RHEL/Fedora	dnf install gnupg2	(gpgv 2.x)

    To create the keyring you also need gpg itself (any version) on some
    machine; the news server only needs gpgv.

				THE KEYRING

    pgpverify uses, in this order:

	1. the keyring given with -k in pgpverifyargs (diablo.config);
	2. path_home/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg (normally /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg)
	   if that file exists;
	3. gpgv's own default, trustedkeys.gpg or trustedkeys.kbx in
	   ~news/.gnupg (pgpverify runs gpgv with HOME=path_home).

    Use (2): one file, owned by root or news, readable by news.

    The keyring must be in the classic OpenPGP keyring format.  gpgv 1.x
    cannot read GnuPG 2.x's "keybox" format (.kbx).  The format gpg
    writes with --export is a classic keyring, so build the file by
    importing the keys into a scratch GnuPG home and exporting them:

	# as root, with gpg (1.x or 2.x)
	mkdir -m 755 /news/pgp
	tmp=$(mktemp -d)
	gpg --homedir "$tmp" --import PGPKEYS
	gpg --homedir "$tmp" --list-keys --with-fingerprint
	    # compare the fingerprints with the published ones!
	gpg --homedir "$tmp" --export > /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg
	chown news:news /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg
	chmod 644 /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg
	rm -rf "$tmp"

    Check what it holds:

	gpg --no-default-keyring --keyring /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg --list-keys

    and test it with a real signed control message, see TESTING below.

    Only keys in this file are trusted: a message signed by any other key
    is rejected, whatever it claims to be.

			    WHERE TO GET THE KEYS

    The keys of the hierarchies administered through ISC (comp, misc,
    news, rec, sci, soc, talk, humanities) and many others are collected
    in PGPKEYS.  INN's control.ctl, also maintained by ISC, lists who may
    send which control message and which key signs it:

	https://ftp.isc.org/pub/pgpcontrol/PGPKEYS
	https://ftp.isc.org/pub/usenet/CONFIG/control.ctl

    Hierarchies that publish their own key say so in control.ctl, usually
    with a URL.  Always check key fingerprints against a second source.

    ISC sends each of its control messages twice, a second apart: one
    copy signed with its current key (news.announce.newgroups, key id
    FAFE7B550C18C8B7, in PGPKEYS) and one with its older key (key id
    C25D3AD3B88DA9C1), which PGPKEYS no longer carries.  With a current
    keyring the older copy is rejected ("pgp signature bad, or signer key
    not in keyring") and the other copy is executed.  That is expected.

			    DCONTROL.CTL EXAMPLES

    The word after "verify-" must equal the first word of the signing
    key's user id.  The key that signs for news.announce.newgroups has
    the user id "news.announce.newgroups", so:

	newgroup:group-admin@isc.org:comp.*|misc.*|news.*:verify-news.announce.newgroups
	rmgroup:group-admin@isc.org:comp.*|misc.*|news.*:verify-news.announce.newgroups
	checkgroups:group-admin@isc.org:*:verify-news.announce.newgroups=miscctl

    "=name" also logs the message and the result to log/name (here
    /news/log/miscctl).  The
    sender pattern (group-admin@isc.org) is checked first, the signature
    second; both must match.  See samples/dcontrol.ctl.

    To find a key's first user-id word:

	gpg --no-default-keyring --keyring /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg --list-keys

			    DIABLO.CONFIG EXAMPLES

    Nothing is needed when gpgv is in one of the directories above and
    the keyring is /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg.  Otherwise:

	# a different keyring
	pgpverifyargs	-k /etc/news/pgpkeys.gpg

	# a specific gpgv (e.g. gpgv1 although gpgv 2.x is installed)
	pgpverifyargs	-k /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg /usr/bin/gpgv1

	# PGP 2.x, the old behaviour (keyring in ~news/.pgp)
	pgpverifyargs	/usr/local/bin/pgp -f

    Restart dreaderd after changing diablo.config.  The keyring is read
    for every message, and dreaderd picks up dcontrol.ctl changes for
    the next control message.

    When a check fails, the control log says why: the X-PGP-Sig header
    is missing; the signature is bad or the signer's key is not in the
    keyring; or the header could not be parsed or gpgv could not be run.

				TESTING

    pgpverify reads one article (headers, blank line, body) on stdin:

	/news/dbin/pgpverify -k /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg < article
	echo $?

    samples/pgp-sample, the test message older documentation suggests,
    is a 1998 checkgroups signed with an ISC key that PGPKEYS no longer
    carries, so it fails against a current keyring.  Use a recent control
    message instead, e.g. from control.checkgroups on a news server.

    It prints the first user-id word of the signer and exits 0 for a
    good signature.  Exit 1: no X-PGP-Sig header; 2: malformed header or
    gpgv not found; 3: bad signature or unknown key.  -d shows the gpgv
    command and its status lines; -d2 also keeps the temporary files
    pgpverify.msg.* and pgpverify.sig.* in $TMPDIR (default /tmp).

				UPDATING KEYS

    Keys expire and are replaced.  Rebuild trustedkeys.gpg from a fresh
    PGPKEYS as above, or add a single key:

	tmp=$(mktemp -d)
	gpg --homedir "$tmp" --import /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg newkey.asc
	gpg --homedir "$tmp" --export > /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg.new
	mv /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg.new /news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg
	rm -rf "$tmp"

    gpgv rejects signatures by expired or revoked keys.

				    PGP 2.x

    When pgpverifyargs names a program called "pgp", pgpverify pipes the
    clear-signed message into it as before (e.g. "pgp -f") and reads
    "Good signature from" from its output.  Keys then live in pgp's own
    keyring (~news/.pgp).  New installations should use gpgv.

			HOW THE SIGNED TEXT IS REBUILT

    For reference, as signcontrol and INN's pgpverify define it
    (ftp.isc.org/pub/pgpcontrol/FORMAT): "X-Signed-Headers: <list>", then
    each listed header as "Name: value" in that order and case (a header
    missing from the article still appears, with an empty value), a blank
    line and the body including its final newline; trailing blanks are
    removed from every line.  The X-PGP-Sig value is "<version> <list>
    <base64 signature>", with the signature continued over folded lines.
